!!! 3.2.1 Étale \(\boldsymbol{\varphi}\)-modules over \(\boldsymbol{E}\).

''Definition 3.15.'' //A// \(\varphi\)-module //over \(E\) is an \(E\)-vector space \(M\) together with a map \(\varphi : M \to M\) which is semi-linear with respect to the absolute Frobenius \(\sigma\), i.e.,//

\[\varphi(x+y) = \varphi(x) + \varphi(y), \quad \textit{for all } x,y \in M; \tag{3.12}\]
\[\varphi(\lambda x) = \sigma(\lambda)\varphi(x) = \lambda^{p}\varphi(x), \quad \textit{for all } \lambda \in E,\ x \in M. \tag{3.13}\]

\(M_\varphi = E \,{}_{\sigma}\!\otimes_{E} M\),

\[
\begin{array}{rrcl}
\varPhi : & M_\varphi & \longrightarrow & M \\
& \lambda \otimes x & \longmapsto & \lambda\varphi(x).
\end{array}
\]

''Proposition 3.17.'' //If \(M\) is a \(\varphi\)-module of finite dimension \(d\), then//

\[
\begin{aligned}
\varPhi\ \textit{is an isomorphism} &\iff \varPhi\ \textit{is injective} \iff \varPhi\ \textit{is surjective} \\
&\iff M = E \cdot \varphi(M) \iff A = (a_{ij}) \in \mathrm{GL}_d(E).
\end{aligned}
\]

''Definition 3.18.'' //A \(\varphi\)-module \(M\) over \(E\) is called// étale //if \(\varPhi : M_\varphi \to M\) is an isomorphism and if \(\dim_E M\) is finite.//

Let \(\mathscr{M}^{\text{ét}}_{\varphi}(E)\) be the category of étale \(\varphi\)-modules over \(E\) with the morphisms being the \(E\)-linear maps which commute with \(\varphi\).

From §1.2.4, we let \(\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{E}}\) be the Cohen ring \(\mathcal{C}(E)\) of \(E\) and \(\mathcal{E}\) be the field of fractions of \(\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{E}}\). Then

\[\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{E}} = \varprojlim_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{E}}/p^n\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{E}}\]

and \(\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{E}}/p\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{E}} = E\), \(\mathcal{E} = \mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{E}}[\frac{1}{p}]\).

To fix ideas we choose an algebraic closure \(\overline{\mathbb{Q}_p}\) of \(\mathbb{Q}_p\). The absolute value \(|\ |\) on \(\mathbb{C}_p\) will be normalized by \(|\boldsymbol{\pi}| = q^{-1}\). Whenever we refer to a topology on \(\mathbb{C}_p\), it will be the one induced by the metric \(|\ |\).

We consider now any intermediate field \(L \subseteq K \subseteq \mathbb{C}_p\) which is //perfectoid//, i.e., which satisfies:

* \(K\) is complete,
* the value group \(|K^{\times}|\) is dense in \(\mathbb{R}^{\times}_{>0}\), and
* \((o_K/po_K)^p = o_K/po_K\).

''Remark 1.4.3'' //Every element of the value group \(|K^{\times}|\) is a pth power.//

//Proof// Let \(a \in K^{\times}\). There is a \(\varpi \in K^{\times}\) such that \(|p| < |\varpi| < 1\) and \(|\varpi^{m+1}| < |a| \leq |\varpi^{m}|\). It follows that \(|a| = |b^{p}| = |b|^{p}\). @@display:block;text-align:right;\(\square\)@@

\[
\begin{aligned}
o_{K^{\flat}} &:= \varprojlim\big(\cdots \xrightarrow{(\cdot)^q} o_K/\varpi o_K \xrightarrow{(\cdot)^q} \cdots \xrightarrow{(\cdot)^q} o_K/\varpi o_K\big) \\
&= \{(\ldots,\alpha_i,\ldots,\alpha_1,\alpha_0) \in (o_K/\varpi o_K)^{\mathbb{N}_0} : \alpha_{i+1}^{q} = \alpha_i \text{ for any } i \geq 0\}.
\end{aligned}
\]

''Lemma 1.4.6'' //The map//

\[
\begin{array}{rcl}
|\ |_{\flat} : o_{K^{\flat}} & \longrightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \\
\alpha & \longmapsto & |\alpha^{\sharp}|
\end{array}
\]

//is a nonarchimedean absolute value. Furthermore, we have://

(i) \(|o_{K^{\flat}}|_{\flat} = |o_K|\).
(ii) \(\alpha o_{K^{\flat}} \subseteq \beta o_{K^{\flat}}\)//, for any \(\alpha,\beta \in o_{K^{\flat}}\), if and only if \(|\alpha|_{\flat} \leq |\beta|_{\flat}\).//

''Definition 1.3.2'' A Frobenius power series (for \(\pi\)) is a formal power series \(\phi(X) \in o[[X]]\) such that

\[\phi(X) = \pi X + \text{higher terms} \quad \text{and} \quad \phi(X) \equiv X^q \bmod \pi o[[X]].\]

''Example'' Let \(L = \mathbb{Q}_p\), \(\pi = p\), and \(\phi(X) = (1+X)^p - 1\) so that \(F_{\phi} = \widehat{\mathbb{G}}_m\). Then

\[\mathscr{F}_n = \{\zeta - 1 : \zeta^{p^n} = 1\} \qquad \text{and} \qquad L_n = L(\{\zeta : \zeta^{p^n} = 1\}).\]

On the other hand we define on the set \(W(B)_L := B^{\mathbb{N}_0}\) a new ‘addition’

\[(a_n)_n \boxplus (b_n)_n := (S_n(a_0,\ldots,a_n,b_0,\ldots,b_n))_n\]

''Proposition 1.4.12'' //The completion// \(\hat{L}_{\infty}\) //of the field// \(L_{\infty}\) //in// (1.3.9) //is perfectoid.//

This is another displayed equation $$e=mc^2$$ and a ProperWikiWord, a ~NotAWikiWord and a [[pretty link|Lubin–Tate formal group law]].

perfectoid fields \(\hat{L}_{\infty} \subseteq K \subseteq \mathbb{C}_p\) \(\longrightarrow\) complete and perfect fields \(\hat{L}_{\infty}^{\flat} \subseteq F \subseteq \mathbb{C}_p^{\flat}\)

\[(K, |\ |) \longmapsto (K^{\flat}, |\ |_{\flat})\,. \tag{1.4.18}\]
